# Horsehair Jack

**Ingredients:** WEST AFRICAN GOJE + LEFTFIELD / JACKING HOUSE + PREPARED CLAVICHORD + CALABASH PERCUSSION + BASS TROMBONE + SHORT ACID BASS

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a dry-season residency inside a retired grain-cleaning station outside Kano, where goje player Amina Sani was recording bowed one-string phrases in a concrete sorting bay while Manchester producer Theo Marr programmed stripped jack-house patterns in a disused control room. The breakthrough came when Theo stopped treating Amina’s bow changes as expressive decoration and began writing sections around the moment friction itself changed direction. A down-bow carried one grain of noise, an up-bow another; when the electronic rhythm stayed constant, those opposing bow surfaces made identical pitches feel like alternating harmonic states. Prepared clavichord, calabash percussion, bass trombone, and a short acid-bass voice were added to build a club form where timbral reversal matters as much as chord change.

Horsehair Jack is a dry acoustic-electronic dance form built around directional bow friction. Leftfield and jacking house provide the chassis: 118–126 BPM, compact four-on-the-floor kick, syncopated claps, terse shuffled hats, short bass cells, acidic filter motion, and long repetitive sections that increase pressure by articulation and mute changes rather than by huge drops. The goje supplies a contrasting physical logic through a single bowed string, skin-topped resonator, flexible pitch, bow-pressure noise, sliding intonation, and the audible asymmetry between forward and reverse bow travel.

The goje is recorded with one close condenser aimed toward the bow-string contact area, one ribbon microphone near the skin resonator, one removable contact microphone on the body, and one restrained room microphone. The close channel captures horsehair scrape, attack granularity, finger slide, and bow reversal. The ribbon supplies low-mid skin resonance and body depth. The contact channel emphasizes mechanical vibration and muted bow contact. The room microphone remains low so the instrument feels immediate rather than folkloric or cinematic.

Players favor one- to four-note cells, long tonic pedals, minor thirds, fourths, narrow slides, bent seconds, octave suggestions, repeated single-note pulses, scratch tones, muted bow pressure, and sustained notes whose character changes entirely when bow direction reverses. Fast melodic display is rare. The defining gesture is one pitch held through multiple bow changes so the listener hears texture rotate while note identity stays almost fixed.

The central compositional device is the **Friction Ledger**. Every major goje phrase is mapped through four states: DRAW, TURN, RETURN, RELEASE. DRAW is the first bow direction and establishes the core timbre. TURN is the instant direction changes and friction spikes. RETURN is the opposite bow direction, usually carrying a different noise profile despite the same pitch center. RELEASE is the point where bow pressure falls away and the resonator is allowed to decay. Electronic parts are arranged around these states but are not sensor-triggered.

A bass sequence may remain unchanged while repeated DRAW and RETURN strokes alternate above it, making the harmony feel like it is breathing between two surfaces. In another section the kick may drop out only during TURN states, making the short scrape at bow reversal function as a transient without becoming a literal drum hit. The compositional identity comes from directional friction, not from pitch tracking.

House drums remain dry and muscular. Kicks are short and rounded, usually every quarter note, with occasional one-beat omissions. Claps are clipped and can sit slightly late. Hats use shuffled eighths and sparse sixteenths with deliberate vacancies around exposed goje bow changes. Toms are minimal. The rhythm should feel bodily and insistent rather than glossy.

Acid bass is restrained and concise. A mono resonant synth uses one- to three-note patterns, roots, fifths, octave jumps, and occasional semitone approaches. Filter resonance can rise over sixteen or thirty-two bars, but screaming 303 solos are excluded. The bass must leave enough low-mid space for the goje skin resonator and bass trombone.

Prepared clavichord supplies dry middle-register punctuation. Felt, paper, and removable damping soften selected strings while others retain their metallic tangent attack. The player uses clipped fifths, minor sevenths, suspended seconds, single-note repetitions, and short octave figures. Clavichord entries often occur after RELEASE states, creating a fixed-pitch reply after the unstable bowed texture disappears.

Calabash percussion provides hollow acoustic impact. Dry palm strikes, fingertip taps, shell knocks, muted slaps, and hand scrapes replace selected electronic percussion events. Continuous decorative patterns are avoided. The calabash should sound like a physical chamber responding to the same dry room as the goje.

Bass trombone occupies the low-mid register with short fundamentals, muted fourths, breath-led attacks, plunger-muted pulses, and brief falls. It enters mainly when the acid bass is absent, preventing excessive low-frequency accumulation. Long brass swells and cinematic scoring are excluded.

Vocals are optional and concise. Dry spoken lines may use Hausa or English fragments describing belt numbers, grain grades, sluice settings, dust readings, axle counts, and simple physical actions. Large sung hooks are not required. When voice appears, it remains subordinate to rhythm and friction.

Effects are narrow and controlled. The principal goje signal stays close and mostly dry. One short mono slap can be fed from selected TURN events. Clavichord may use a dark plate. Bass trombone receives a short room. Acid bass stays mono and nearly effect-free. Reverb tails must never smear the difference between DRAW and RETURN.

The defining tension is stable machine pulse versus unstable bow surface. The pitch may remain almost unchanged while the physical texture keeps flipping direction underneath it.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 118–126 BPM, usually 4/4, with compact jacking-house kick, clipped clap, shuffled hats, occasional kick omissions, and long goje strokes crossing several beats.

**Harmonic Palette:** Dorian and Aeolian centers, open fourths and fifths, minor thirds, suspended 2nds, narrow semitone bends, tonic pedals, occasional tritone color, and long sections where timbral change substitutes for chord progression.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Friction Ledger** — goje phrases move through DRAW → TURN → RETURN → RELEASE, with directional bow texture treated as formal material.
2. **Same-Pitch Reversal** — repeated notes retain the same pitch center while bow direction changes enough to alter perceived harmonic color.
3. **Turn Vacancy** — selected kick, clap, or hat events are removed around bow reversals so friction spikes become audible without sampling them as drums.
4. **Release Clavichord** — prepared clavichord enters mainly after bowed RELEASE states, supplying short fixed-pitch punctuation.
5. **Calabash Substitution** — acoustic shell impacts replace selected machine percussion rather than layering continuously.
6. **No-Wall Rule** — intensity is never achieved through massive pads, guitar layers, or reverb buildup; pressure comes from repetition, filter motion, friction, and low-frequency restraint.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through stronger bow pressure, faster directional reversals, denser shuffled hats, increased acid-filter resonance, harder calabash attacks, brighter clavichord tangents, and more forceful bass-trombone entries. Peaks remain dry, percussive, and close.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Horsehair Jack formed inside a decommissioned grain-cleaning and bagging station outside Kano. The building retained corrugated elevators, belt housings, wooden grain chutes, faded weighing charts, steel inspection ladders, woven sacks, red dust-control labels, concrete sorting bays, and broad floor drains that carried fine dust toward an old sluice channel.

Goje player **Amina Sani** had been using one bay to document bow response under different horsehair tensions. She became interested in the fact that a sustained note did not sound identical when the bow direction reversed, even if finger position and pitch remained almost unchanged. The friction profile, attack density, and skin resonance shifted enough to create the sensation of two timbral states.

Producer **Theo Marr** was working nearby on stripped leftfield house. His first attempt cut Amina’s notes into neat one-beat samples. The result sounded efficient but erased the bow itself. He restored the continuous performance, kept one four-on-the-floor pattern unchanged, and muted the hats every time Amina reversed direction. Suddenly the room’s smallest physical event became the loudest rhythmic detail.

Prepared-clavichord player **Zainab Musa** joined after designing short metallic replies that could appear only after bowed decay. Calabash percussionist **Idris Bello** replaced programmed rims and auxiliary hits with hollow shell strikes. Bass-trombonist **Martha Vale** completed the group with low acoustic weight that could enter whenever Theo removed the acid bass.

Their first performances were called **friction readings**. A narrow slate beside Amina displayed four words: DRAW, TURN, RETURN, RELEASE. Nothing on stage was automated by the chart. It simply described what listeners were meant to notice inside one bow cycle.

The visual language developed around grain-dust beige, faded industrial red, galvanized steel, matte black, woven-fiber brown, chalk white, and weak green inspection lamps. The unofficial rule is that a bow reversal should remain audible even when the dancefloor is full.

